reach and  how that intersects rather conveniently  with your handlers past you're  immediately whisked back to the lands  and territories from the base game to  cull a couple of monsters that have  gotten too big for their boots it's not  exactly narrative whiplash but  certainly not as compelling as it would  have been have we been the ones to  follow the handler from the first step  of her journey to the new lands of  ice-born that said revisiting those  familiar locations early on to take down  new and improved versions of killer  dinosaurs that you've faced before it's  one of the most compelling parts of the  game as with the DLC strategy the  building blocks of the expansion of  steeped in elements that existing  players are already acquainted with the  changes that ice-born makes in the form  of these variant breeds has a two-fold  effect first they provide you with the  motivation to form new strategies to  slice and dice their way to the next  story beat secondly just distinct enough  in terms of attack patterns and  additional elemental considerations that  you never really feel like you're just  fighting a reskin of something you made  mincemeat out of 80 hours ago it's as if  there's been a concerted effort to  balance the difficulty of what many fans  might rightfully view as the second  coming of the coveted G rank in this  latest iteration  despite some focus on iterating  established systems that are innovations  introduced in ice-born that truly set it  apart from its predecessor brand new  monsters and the implementation of  legacy series favorites look and feel  impressive thanks to all the new ways  monsters can interact with other beasts  and the various location  but more importantly there is a  library's worth of new weapon moves for  you to take advantage of charge acts  users can now cancel into a particularly  fun multi-directional attack when caught  unawares and gun Lance users no longer  have to worry about running out of ammo  in an emergency before getting to use  their new signature move that is quite  literally explosive hunting-horn users  will also have had their ability to do  damage buffed with the addition of a new  move that lets you stick your horn in  the ground and spin it like some kind of  bizarre beyblade to catch whatever is  charging towards you off-guard that's  just a few new examples but overall  these additions seem to be informed by  the dual precepts of style and lethality  however the biggest quality-of-life  addition has been something called the  clutch claw every hunter can use this  alongside their primary weapon to  grapple onto their foe and depending on  what other sharp object is equipped do  anything from steering a rampaging wave  and into a rock face to weakening a  specific part of its body that needs to  be lopped off having the option and  ability to scale up the side of a  monster after landing a savvy shot with  the clutch cloth feels ridiculously  satisfying and the surge is driving a  beast to its doom in the many  treacherous terrain pitfalls that dot  hoarfrost reach that new frosty  landscape is beautiful and treacherous  in equal measure  with new foes more verticality than the  Kuril Highlands and frozen terrain that  can crack and send you plunging to your  death it really is a sight to behold  more than ever it feels like the  environment can be turned against you  some monsters will uproot trees and  throw them at you while the wrong move  on cracked ice can mean certain death  having a grasp of every bit of the map  is integral to truly mastering what ice  one has to throw at you and it's  incredibly satisfying when everything  suddenly clicks and you go from the  hunted to the hunter leading their prey  to a painful trap that attempts to even  the scales being dropped into this  intricately designed location as a  relatively new player may be  overwhelming but no matter your  experience level joining up with other  hunters and picking your way through the  icy den of beasts together is incredibly  rewarding in its own way  and iceborn benefits from the  matchmaking improvements introduced  since the release of the base game  Monster Hunter World ice-born is at its  best when you're fighting tooth and nail  against something that you know could  crush you within its teeth in a second  even though this might feel like it came  at the expense of a more interesting  narrative nothing is quite as good as  the biting chill cutting through the  first of your armor the shrill cry of  your palico as it comes to your aid and  the wind roaring in your ears as you  latch on to a beasts flank and climb up  its side while it [ __ ] and roars this  expansion is rife with moments like that  all of the improvements feel like they  were done with the excellent building  blocks of Monster Hunter world in mind  which means that getting to the meat of  the matter is quicker and more  satisfying than ever there's no more  fussing about with the new systems or  worrying about ruffling the feathers of  hardcore fans with the direction change  in the series those teething problems  have already come and gone  I spawn is a confident step into the  future of the franchise and it's hard  not to think about what might come next  thanks for being here you're a lifesaver
 

